  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nOur beautiful weather will continue the rest of today and most\nof tomorrow as high pressure dominates. Tomorrow afternoon a\ncold front will move into northwest Minnesota and from the \nevening to the morning will moves across Minnesota and western \nWisconsin. This will be driving force behind the thunderstorms \nchances. So with ample forcing attention then turns to the \nmesoscale environment, to determine where CI will occur and \nwhere convection can be maintained. The parameter that looks \ngood across the broader region is shear. Hodographs support \nsupercells with forecast soundings showing almost textbook \ncurved hodographs. The HRRR for example has very high amounts \nof SRH in 0-1km of 300 sometimes 400 m2/s2. The limiting factor\nwill be instability especially in the lower levels. There is \nlittle low level instability for us, with the main area of low \nlevel instability staying across northern Minnesota. For this \nreason many of the CAMs that go this far out have not shown much\nconvection for us and they keep the main area to our north \nacross areas covered by WFO FGF and DLH. If we can get storms to\ninitiate then the environment will be favorable for supercells \nwith all modes of severe weather possible. The most likely area\nwill be across central Minnesota where SPC has higher risks as \nwell for all modes. Although with high LCLs, the main risk will \nbe for hail and wind. Rain wise this appears to be a very \nconvectively driven rain signal. So either a lot of rain with a \nthunderstorm or nothing. So most will see no rain.\n\nThis will be a notable cold front with a much colder air mass\nover us this weekend behind frontal passage. Highs on Saturday\nlook to be in the lower 70s and Sunday in the 60s. Overall our\nfirst taste of more fall like weather after the continuation of\nsummer temperatures for the start of September. Sunday into\nMonday though I have some good news as a large, more fall like\nsystem will move into the Upper Midwest. By more fall like I \nmean supportive of widespread stratiform rain rather than \nthunderstorms. As drought has continued to expand across \nMinnesota and western Wisconsin this could provide some much \nneeded relief with a nice soaking rain. Precipitable water \nvalues still look to be 150-200% of normal and this moisture \ncombined with the forcing of a pretty stacked shortwave aloft \nconceptually makes this look like the kind of system that gives\nus a good rain. Looking towards both deterministic and ensemble\nguidance there is strong agreement in the large scale, more\nsynoptic, factors. Basically pretty much unanimous agreement of\nthis occurring across membership, with 98 percent of global\nensemble membership showing widespread rain chances. Where \nthere are differences are where the heaviest QPF sets up. So \nwhile PoPs are and should remain high QPF values are more \nvariable. As we get closer we should be able to hone in better \non where the heavier QPF will fall. Going back to ensemble \nmembership it is when you go up to rainfall of a quarter inch or\nmore is where the spread starts to show. This rain will come \nwith WAA on Monday bringing highs up slightly into the mid 60s \nto mid 70s. Behind the system on Tuesday though cooler air \nreturns with highs falling back into the mid to upper 60s. Mid \nweek looks to trend dry behind the early week rain with a very \ndispersive signal in the models late week. Each deterministic \nglobal model alone, ensembles aside, has a very different \nevolution of the pattern late next week. What is more certain is\nthat the cooler temperatures look likely to continue.",
